I only chose reviews that came out in 1997.

167
00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:10.970
Great.

168
00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:12.210
When the movie came out.

169
00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:16.070
This is from Real Views, Real is spelled R-E-E-L.

170
00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:22.250
Their movie reviewer, James Berardinelli, gave it two stars and said the following.

171
00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:27.230
Frankly, even though The Edge is almost always dumb, it's also often fun.

172
00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.050
And to give the film its due, it's never boring.

173
00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:35.010
On the rare occasion when the plot goes for character development, the performances of

174
00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:36.970
Hopkins and Baldwin carry it through.

175
00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:43.030
The cinematography is frequently breathtaking, and Jerry Goldsmith's score has enough majesty

176
00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:45.290
to highlight the impressive vistas.

177
00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.470
This film includes several memorable moments.

178
00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:53.930
And also, The Edge can boast what is, hands down, the best anti-lawyer line of the year.

179
00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.230
I'm going to bring it up when we get to it.

180
00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:02.830
However, just like creamy icing can't save a half-baked cake, these positive elements

181
00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:06.110
can't hide the fact that The Edge is a bad movie.

182
00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:09.510
That's a two-star review from James.

183
00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:12.330
What does he need the filmmakers to do?

184
00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:13.870
Come and literally suck the s**k?

185
00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:15.630
How do I get two more stars?

186
00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:22.070
There's majesty, cinematography, amazing performances, wonderful dialogue.

187
00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:25.530
What else do you need while you're watching this movie, James?

188
00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:27.210
A literal blow s**k?

189
00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:28.190
Oh my gosh.

190
00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:28.990
What do you need?

191
00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:29.450
Jenna!

192
00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:31.210
Don't come for my movie.

193
00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:32.550
Oh my gosh.

194
00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:38.950
This is like maybe the most hostile I've ever heard you on the podcaSt.

195
00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:40.830
All right.

196
00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:43.890
The next review, I'm only giving you two from 1997.

197
00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:48.310
The next one is from Roger Ebert, who gave it three out of four stars.

198
00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:49.250
Yes.

199
00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:52.270
Roger Ebert, established movie critic.

200
00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.650
You don't know James.

201
00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:56.390
I know James.

202
00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:57.290
Okay.

203
00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:00.870
I know enough about James that it's all I need to know.

204
00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:02.970
Roger wrote a very long review.

205
00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:04.350
I can put a link to it.

206
00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:05.910
But here are the highlights.

207
00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:11.710
He says about The Edge, it's subtly funny in the way it toys with the cliches of the genre.

208
00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:13.750
Too subtle, apparently, for some.

209
00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.210
Like James.

210
00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:23.990
The movie contains glorious scenery, quixotic, mammoth conversations, and of course, the obligatory action scenes.

211
00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:27.910
Even in generating tension, the movie toys with convention.

212
00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:37.310
There are a few bear wrestling matches and a big showdown with the Beast, but the movie doesn't lose its mind and go berserk with action in the last half hour,

213
00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:39.450
as most action films seem to.

214
00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:50.630
It is typical of Mammoth that he could devise his plot in such a way that the climactic payoff would not be bloodshed, but the simple exchange of a wristwatch.

215
00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:52.670
Thank you, Roger Ebert.

216
00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:54.290
Thank you.

217
00:09:54.630 --> 00:10:02.970
It also got 62% on Rotten Tomatoes' tomato meter and 70% on the popcorn meter, which is the fan ranking.

218
00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:03.630
Mm-hmm.

219
00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.910
Maybe I shouldn't have read the reviews.

220
00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:13.930
There's nothing you can say about my movie.

221
00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:15.290
I'm not saying it.

222
00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:20.050
That I will ever, ever stray from my love for it.

223
00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:22.890
I'm reading other people's words.

224
00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:24.010
All right.

225
00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:28.770
And I also thought we should hear one of the original movie trailers for The Edge.

226
00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:30.790
It's that classic trailer.

227
00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:36.010
Remember how in the 90s they all had the same voiceover actor who would say things like,

228
00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:37.950
